[{:seon.task/name "the-judge-reads-run-tests-results"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent's run carries clojure.test's report format end to end: `seon.agent/run-tests-here` collects each assertion as a report map (`seon.agent/test-report`), and the judge and the turn read them back through `seon.proofs/results`, `seon.proofs/red-reports`, `seon.proofs/failures`, `seon.proofs/test-run-errors` and `seon.proofs/costs`, while `seon.proofs/run-tests!` already answers one result per test ({:test :digest :pass :fail :error :ms}, a throw's :message and :data). Correct: the judge (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`'s input), the turn (`seon.turn/observe`), the run's facts (:seon.run/failures, :seon.run/costs) and the context read run-tests!'s result map, each red test keeping its failing assertions (expected, actual, got); the report vector, `test-report` and the readers that rebuild a status per test from it go. About -30 lines in src. Proofs: the done error, the run's failures and costs, and the context's red block read the same from the result map as today from the reports."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "turn-logic-from-recorded-data"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: tests shouldn't be running agent turns. A run that names nothing leaves out the tests and steps that reach a process (test-runner/automatic over seon.program/reach-index's :process?; notes/process-tests-2026-10-23.md), so what they cover is proven only when named, in the full run or at root's gate. Where the covered logic is a function of data, test it without a process: the reconciler's step from a recorded fact state (seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc on a db holding the run facts, as procs-test does), the merge from two dbs (seon.merge/three-way, global-ids' delta-three-way), the context render from a recorded basis (seon.context on a :seon.turn/basis), the agent-proc step from recorded events, seon.server/respond on recorded requests, the help page's and templates' forms through seon.repl's handler in process, the restart catch-up as load-all! over a db holding two merges. Each new unit test names the process test whose logic it carries; the process test stays as the end-to-end check, run when named. Measured: the process tests were 90 of 443 tests and 782 of their 917 s (2026-10-23 full run)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-run-that-names-nothing-leaves-out-what-reaches-a-process}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-plan-keeps-a-program-db-per-tree"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; the owner's option B, notes/test-fork-main-2026-10-23.md). What existed: test-runner/program-db imported the whole of src and test into the in-memory test store on every plan (seon.source/import-tx, then d/db-with): on today's main a plan of tests and steps was 1.94G ticks warm (6.1 s CPU), and transacting the import into LMDB alone 1.43G ticks (4.2 s) even after jolt/datom-cost. The program db is a function of the git tree, so it is kept per tree: one LMDB store per checkout (:seon.config.proofs/program-store, target/program-store), a branch per tree id (seon.fx/tree-id, git's content address, never updated). The same tree reads its branch; a tree never seen is branched from the kept tree with the fewest changed program files (git diff --name-only, trees in the checkout's own repository) and brought current by seon.source/refresh-tx; only an empty store imports the whole program. Every text is read from the tree's git objects (git archive), never the working tree, so an edit after the tree id was taken cannot be kept under it; the diff is --no-renames, so a moved and renamed namespace leaves no old namespace behind. One writer at a time per store (two runs never share a checkout). Bounded in bytes (:seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes): an LMDB file never shrinks, so past the bound the store is removed before it is opened and starts again from the run's tree. A new tree's nearest kept tree costs one git diff per kept tree (a few ms each). Measured: d/branch! 2 ms, a one-file refresh-tx 6 ms and its transact 23 ms; the store 12 MB for the whole program, ~2 MB more a kept tree with one changed file; a kept tree's program db 1.17M ticks and 4.6 MB whatever the program's size (the cost proof); bin/test's selection 1.65 s on an unchanged tree and 1.7 s after a one-file edit, against ~15 s with an empty store. What is left of a plan is the reach graph (~240M ticks, ~1 s). Tests that built a whole program db (program-test, agent-test, main-test) take this process's own store (seon.test-store/program-store). Deletes the per-run import and the runner's note that a fork cannot connect to a store. Option A, a worktree's root answering through seon_prove, stays the end state: this is its tree-to-branch step."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-plan-reads-the-kept-program-db-of-its-tree
                      seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-runs-the-binary-through-binary-sh"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink session): seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-its-keys-under-their-current-names runs bin/ports, which runs ./seon ports, through clojure.java.shell/sh directly, not through seon.test-store/binary-sh, the one call seon.program/reach-index's :binary? counts (:seon.config.source/binary-vars). So bin/test's plan does not build target/seon for it, and on a worktree whose binary is another tree's it is red for a reason that is not the change (config refused: unknown keys). Correct: every test that runs the binary or a script that runs it (bin/root, bin/check, bin/ports, bin/mcp, ./seon) does so through binary-sh, and a law over test/'s forms (read as data) is red on a shell call naming one of them another way, as seon.check-test/every-test-root-starts-through-start-root! is for roots."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-prove-runs-key-does-not-move-between-two-asks"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (owner's ruling 2026-10-24: a prove run is keyed by the digest of what it reads). Cause: main's tx moved between the two asks by the run's own writes, its results and then its prove agent's end facts (status, ended-ms), after the tx seon.prove remembered (:after), so the second ask spawned a second run. A run's key is now branch@digest of what it reads: main's, the checkout's seon.fx/inputs (tree and env) and main's program (seon.program/program-digest, new: it replaces the :after rule, deleted); an agent's, its branch's program digest, which seon.root/prove! and seon.agent/prove! take of the same head (an agent's tx moves too: the eval that asked records itself on its branch). Any file change in the checkout (docs too) or change to main's program moves main's key, and a re-plan reuses every green; coarser than the per-test digests because it must be known before admission, which those cannot be without the run's own planning (spawn and load of the tests, 9-14 s measured). A repeat ask of main: 13.7 s -> 0.48 s. docs/merging.md#the-test-service. Original report: Reproduction: Found 2026-10-23 by the cutover's decider: red in a built scratch worktree of main 4bc598dc1 as well as in the fresh clone, so it is main's, not the cutover's. `bin/test --all seon.prove-test`: `seon.prove-test/a-session-prove-of-main-runs-in-a-prove-agent-and-leaves-facts` (test/seon/prove_test.clj:157-158) asks the same prove twice and expects the same run: the first key is `main@536871112:seon.patterns-test`, the second `main@536871152:seon.patterns-test`, and two run facts exist where one is expected. Cause to find: the key's number (a transaction/entity id of main) moved between the two asks, so the run is not recognised as the same one. Correct: the key names the run by what it is run for (the digest of its inputs), never by a counter that moves when anything else is written; asked again with nothing changed the answer is the same run and nothing runs. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-session-prove-of-main-runs-in-a-prove-agent-and-leaves-facts seon.prove-test/a-run-is-keyed-by-what-it-reads seon.prove-test/the-read-digest-moves-with-what-the-run-reads seon.prove-test/a-session-and-an-agents-turn-for-one-digest-end-in-one-run seon.program-test/the-program-digest-moves-with-the-program-and-not-with-data seon.program-cost-test/the-program-digest-is-one-read-of-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-law-over-source-runs-in-prove"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the full run of main 888a728e2: three reds (seon.main-test/no-docstring-carries-a-date, seon.main-test/only-measure-reads-the-tick-counter, seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed) merged because bin/prove leaves out every test seon.program/graph-index flags :process?. The check-test one starts a seed, a real process. The main-test laws only read the compiler's facts of the tree, yet reach one: measured on the reach graph (a BFS from each test's form over :adj to a form in :proc), every law in main-test (also a-runs-lifecycle-is-read-once, a-bound-is-a-config-key) is one edge from `test-runner/program-db`, which calls clojure.java.shell/sh (a :seon.config.source/process-vars var) for `git cat-file`, `rm -rf` of an oversize store and the git archive import (tree-files). A second route, seon.test-store/program-store -> program-store-path's shutdown hook (sh), flags it too. Not the ns form, and not the :reads edge (seon.program/names-only). Every test calling program-db (program-test, proofs-test, test-store, agent-test) is left out for the same reason. Correct: the program db of the tree is built once by the runner's plan (test-runner/tests-of already builds it) and a test reads that value, not a shell; the test's digest still takes the tree (the :reads flag), so a law reruns when the source changes. Splitting program-db into a no-sh open and a shelling import is weaker (a test cannot know the branch exists). Coordinate with the printer session's reach edge (seon.program/names-only, the :reads flag). Proof: bin/prove's plan line counts the main-test laws among the tests to run, not left out; seon.proofs-test/a-run-that-names-nothing-leaves-out-what-reaches-a-process still holds for a test that starts a root."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "mains-verification-goes-first"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's ruling (2026-10-26): main's merge verification goes first, as a fact the tools read, never a rule people remember, a timer or a poll. Seen today: main's merge verification waited an hour for the load to drop, heavy runs queued on a sleep-poll every session copied (the audit's item 10), and merge lines were typed by hand, some green over a red exit (item 5), with process tests named by hand (item 7b). Built: bin/prove --merge holds an exclusive flock on the main checkout's target/main-verify.lock for its whole run; every other bin/test, bin/check and bin/build calls bin/env's main_first, a blocking flock released at once, so it waits only while main's verification runs and says whom it waits for. The command runs the plan's :merge mode (every key with no green at its digest, process keys too), bin/check contracts and smoke, ends on MERGE OK / MERGE FAILED from their exit statuses (merge_verdict) and appends its line to notes/merge-log.md (the counts, the verdicts, the reds, the src delta, the keys left out against main's last line). docs/merging.md#main-goes-first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-heavy-run-waits-while-mains-verification-runs seon.check-test/a-merges-verdict-is-its-exit-statuses-and-it-writes-its-line}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reads-its-own-recorded-results"
  :seon.task/plan "Left over from task seon.proofs/a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused (merged 52b374f02). `seon.agent/test-plan` reuses the results the agent's own db holds (`seon.proofs/stored-results` over its branch), and the test service records a run's results on main (`seon.prove/result-tx`), so a branch sees main's results only as of its last merge of main. A red the agent's own turn just recorded is on main and not on its branch, so the judge at the same run's end (mode :judge, `seon.agent/prove!` since task seon.reconcile/the-judge-asks-the-test-service) runs it again; a green is reused from the results directory, a red never. Correct: the plan reads results from where the service writes them, main's newest value, not the branch's. Two candidates, to price first: (a) the service answers the agent's run with its result facts, and the agent applies them to its branch in the same commit as its answer (a write of facts true anywhere: they key by digest); (b) the agent's plan reads `stored-results` from main's current head, which the agent does not hold today (the fork server's replica is main at each commit: `seon.agent/base-graph`'s source). No clock, no second store of results. Proof: a turn whose proof is red, then the judge at the same digest: the judge forks nothing and reports the red with its failures, marked :reused."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-judge-reuses-the-red-its-turn-recorded}}]
